---
/**
 * /privacy — plain-language, first-person, and only claims what the site
 * actually does (Phase 7, BUILD-PLAN.md §7). Linked from the footer, not the
 * main nav (the 6-item cap holds). If analytics is ever enabled
 * (SITE.analytics in consts.ts), the analytics section below must be updated
 * in the same change — it currently states, truthfully, that none runs.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { SITE } from "../consts";
---

<Base
  title="Privacy"
  description="What this site does and doesn't collect. Short version: almost nothing."
>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-2xl space-y-6 p-8">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold">Privacy</h1>
    <p class="text-ink">
      Short version: this site collects almost nothing, and we'd like to keep it that way.
    </p>

    <div class="space-y-2">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold">Analytics & cookies</h2>
      <p class="text-ink">
        {
          SITE.analytics
            ? "This site uses GoatCounter for basic visit counts — it sets no cookies, collects no personal data, and keeps only aggregate numbers (which pages were visited, roughly when, from what country)."
            : "This site currently runs no analytics and sets no cookies. If that ever changes, it will be a privacy-respecting counter (no cookies, no personal data, aggregate page counts only) — and this page will say so."
        }
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="space-y-2">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold">Embedded video</h2>
      <p class="text-ink">
        Where the site embeds a video from YouTube, nothing is loaded from YouTube — and no
        request of any kind is made to them — until you click play. Embeds then load through
        YouTube's reduced-tracking domain (youtube-nocookie.com). YouTube's own privacy policy
        applies from the moment you press play.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="space-y-2">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold">Contact</h2>
      {/* AUDIT-FIX (2026-08-03): this used to describe the old lead-gen
          contact form's data flow, but that form was retired (PUB-B,
          2026-07-27) — /contact is a mailto link now, and the only form left
          on the site is the newsletter signup (FollowTheStories), which is
          real but not yet live (no provider chosen — see that component's own
          DISABLED-STATE CONTRACT). Rewritten to describe what's actually
          here. ⚠ OPERATOR READ-APPROVAL REQUIRED — revised sentence
          (staging-only until read). */}
      <p class="text-ink">
        If you email us, we have your email — that's it, and we use it to reply. The newsletter
        signup ("Follow the Stories," in the footer and at /contact) isn't live yet; once it is, it
        will send only your email address to whichever provider we've picked, solely so we can send
        you new dispatches. Nothing else is collected, and you can unsubscribe anytime.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="space-y-2">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold">Hosting</h2>
      <p class="text-ink">
        This site is a static site served by GitHub Pages. Like any web host, GitHub may keep
        standard server logs (IP addresses, requested pages) to operate the service — see
        GitHub's privacy statement for how they handle those.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="space-y-2">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold">Our publishing tool</h2>
      <p class="text-ink">
        We publish our own photography and film to our own social channels (YouTube, Instagram,
        Facebook, TikTok, and similar) using a small self-hosted tool that runs on our own
        computer. The platforms require us to say how that tool handles data, so: it stores the
        login tokens for our own accounts locally, sends each post's content only to the platform
        it's being published to, and collects no information about anyone else — no audience
        data, no follower data, nothing. It has no users besides us, and we can revoke its access
        at any time from each platform's settings. When a platform's API requires media to be
        fetched from a public link, the file for that post is briefly hosted at a public URL and
        can be removed after publishing.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="space-y-2">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold">Your data, your call</h2>
      <p class="text-ink">
        You can ask what we hold about you (for most people: at most an email thread), ask us to
        correct it, or ask us to delete it. Email us and we will, unless a legal obligation
        requires keeping it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">
      Questions? <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`} class="link">{SITE.email}</a>
    </p>
    <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">Last updated July 28, 2026.</p>
  </section>
</Base>
